# -- The corpora are FETCHED, never vendored -----------------------------------
# This repo carries `corpora/corpora.toml` -- names, sources and PINNED refs --
# plus `scripts/fetch_corpora.py` to materialise it. The code itself belongs to
# other people and is not ours to redistribute; a local corpus is a git worktree
# of a repo already on the machine, and a public one is a clone recreatable from
# the manifest at any time.
#
# ! Ignore the SUBDIRECTORIES, not `corpora/` itself, or the manifest and the
# fetch script disappear with them.
corpora/*/

# Eval output. A run writes a report per corpus; the findings that matter get
# written up in `evidence/` deliberately, by hand.
evals/results/
evals/**/scratch/

# -- Ordinary Python -----------------------------------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/
*.egg-info/
.ruff_cache/
.pytest_cache/

# -- Editors and platforms -----------------------------------------------------
.vscode/
.idea/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# !! `uv.lock` IS TRACKED SINCE 2026-08-22, and was ignored on a premise that
# expired. The rule read *"this is not a package and has no dependencies to
# pin"*; it now pins `ruff` and `ty`, which are the gates. Roy: *"we can't have
# my personal computer's `ty` happens to work."*
#
# ! The `==` pins in `pyproject.toml` already fix those two exactly -- both are
# standalone binaries with no Python dependencies of their own -- so the lock
# adds little today. It is tracked anyway, because the day a gate acquires a
# transitive dependency is not a day anyone will remember to revisit this line.

# Subagent-driven-development scratch: one workspace per plan, holding that
# plan's ledger, task briefs, implementer reports and review packages. The
# ledger is the recovery map across a compaction; git history is the record
# that outlives it, so none of this is committed.
.superpowers/

# -- Evidence is OPT-IN --------------------------------------------------------
# !! A run in flight writes into `evidence/<name>/` for as long as it lasts, and
# a `git add -A` from another session sweeps that half-written state into a
# commit. Measured 2026-08-17: it happened twice in one afternoon, from the
# session that had already said it would stop doing it.
#
# So everything directly under `evidence/` is ignored and each directory is
# added back BY NAME, which makes tracking a package a decision rather than an
# accident. ! The directory itself must be un-ignored, not its contents: git
# does not descend into an excluded directory, so `!evidence/x/*` alone matches
# nothing.
evidence/*
!evidence/ga/
!evidence/cycle-0.2.3/
!evidence/comment-review-skill-023-dev-review/
!evidence/rename-left-history-in-the-comments/
!evidence/the-loop-measured-2026-08-27/
!evidence/harness-first-two-arm-run/
!evidence/the-judge-disagreed-with-itself/
!evidence/*.md

# Where a graded run is driven from and what it leaves behind: the file under
# review extracted at its START ref, the `.cmd` that carries the invocation, and
# each `t50-N.json`. ! The MEASUREMENTS are promoted to `evidence/` by hand and
# by name, which is the same opt-in the block above describes -- what stays here
# is the scaffolding, which is re-derivable from a ref and a path.
reviewer-testing/

# CodeGraph index -- derived from the tree, rebuilt with `codegraph init`.
# 4.2 MB and regenerated in under a second, so it is not worth versioning.
.codegraph/

# Scratch. A REVIEWING AGENT writes its probe scripts here -- 2026-08-20 two of
# them left 20+ files under `.tmp/` while this repo's own habit is `git add -A`,
# which would have committed another agent's working notes as though they were
# the work.
.tmp/

# `todo_tool` writes `TODO/.<name>.md.tmp<pid>` and renames it into place. A run
# that dies mid-write leaves one behind, and `git add -A` commits it -- measured
# 2026-08-23, a 284-line temp copy of a TODO landed in a commit. ! `.tmp/` above
# does not cover this: that is a directory, this is a dotted suffix.
TODO/.*.md.tmp*

# Session worktrees. Roy, 2026-08-23: two sessions committing on one branch
# collide, so a second session works in a worktree and fast-forwards to pick the
# branch up -- which means the worktree itself must never be tracked.
.claude/.worktrees/
.claude/worktrees/

# Coverage's own scratch. ! THE DATA FILE, NOT THE PIN: `coverage==7.15.4` is in
# `pyproject.toml` and `uv.lock` is tracked, so what a run MEASURES is
# reproducible. `.coverage` is a binary SQLite file rewritten by every run, and
# `.coverage.*` is what parallel runs leave behind.
.coverage
.coverage.*
htmlcov/
